While graph neural networks (GNNs) have garnered significant recent attention, their ability to process data on arbitrary graph structures remains underutilized in practice, partly due to concerns regarding differentially private (DP) training. We conduct an empirical study to assess the feasibility of deploying DP-GNNs across multiple scenarios. Our results indicate that although adding noise to ensure differential privacy degrades model utility, careful hyperparameter tuning can yield GNNs with meaningful performance on real-world datasets. Furthermore, we analyze the impact of different differentially private mechanisms and find that the Gaussian mechanism generally outperforms the discrete mechanism, with Gaussian mechanism-based models achieving higher accuracy and lower training loss. Although privacy constraints reduce the accuracy of label predictions, they can still effectively support downstream tasks like classification. Ultimately, we contend that despite utility reductions caused by privacy restrictions, it is both feasible and practical to train differentially private GNNs.

