The research into whether and how online content curation systems influence news consumption and, consequently, impact society has gained significant traction in recent years. A predominant approach in this field involves comparing actual outcomes with a baseline of unconstrained news availability. However, a critical limitation of this method is the unrealistic assumption that all curation mechanisms equally facilitate access to the entire news landscape. This paper introduces a refined framework for analyzing news consumption effects that distinguishes between various types of content curation. To validate the utility of this new framework, an empirical section is included that evaluates its application using a broad spectrum of alternative baseline news environments. The discussion then highlights the broader implications of these contributions for the field of news consumption research and the essential data and methodology needed to execute such studies. Notably, the findings from this specific empirical application serve as a cautionary tale, illustrating how empirical results regarding the social impacts of content curation can yield contradictory conclusions when employing the traditional framework versus the proposed new one.

